window.EF_CRUX_DOC_PAGES=["FISCAL POLICY & REVENUE — FULL MIND MAP\n                                   Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Union Budget — Constitutional Framework & Recent Priorities\n 2. GST — Structure, Council, Input Tax Credit & Administration\n 3. Fiscal Policy — Authority, Objectives, Instruments & Stimulus\n 4. Budget Preparation, Economic Survey & Budgeting Methods\n 5. Public Receipts & Expenditure — Revenue/Capital Classification\n 6. Fiscal Deficit — Control Measures & Historical Targets\n 7. Deficit Concepts — Fiscal, Revenue, Primary & Effective Revenue\n 8. Government Borrowing, Deficit Financing & Subsidies\n 9. Parliamentary Budget Procedure, Motions & Budgetary Control\n 10. FRBM Act & Fiscal Responsibility Rules\n 11. Disinvestment Targets & Railway Budget\n 12. Direct Tax Policy, DTC, Equalisation Levy, Capital Gains & Tax-GDP\n 13. Union Tax Revenue, Tax Sharing & Centre-State Tax Powers\n 14. MAT, MODVAT, CENVAT, VAT & Service Tax\n 15. Direct vs Indirect Taxes, Miscellaneous Taxes & Disclosure Schemes\n 16. Finance Commission — Constitutional Role, 15th & 16th Commissions\n 17. Finance Commissions — 14th to 1st & Tax Devolution\n 18. Tax Reform Committees, Tax Relief, PAN & Regional Tax Holidays\n 19. Local Finance, Fiscal Stimulus & NHAI Financing\n\n\n 1. Union Budget — Constitutional Framework & Recent Priorities\n\n• Constitution ke Article 112(1) ke under President har financial year ke estimated receipts aur expenditure ka statement Parliament ke dono\n Houses ke saamne rakhwata hai; ise Annual Financial Statement kehte hain. [Q1]\n• Union Budget ke saath Macro Economic Framework Statement Parliament mein FRBM Act, 2003 ke Section 3(5) ke under present kiya\n jaata hai. [Q2]\n• India mein Gender Budgeting Union Budget 2005-06 se introduce hui. Iska aim policies aur programmes mein gender perspective include\n karke women ko development benefits mein equitable share dena hai. [Q3]\n• Union Budget 2024-25 ki nine 'Viksit Bharat' priorities opportunities create karne par focused thi. 'Sustainable Development' separate\n named priority nahi thi; agriculture productivity/resilience, employment & skilling, inclusive HR development/social justice, manufacturing &\n services etc. priorities mein shamil the. [Q4]\n• Union Budget 2017-18 ke ten main themes mein 'Export Performance' separate theme nahi tha. Budget 2026-27 1 February 2026 ko\n present hua aur Kartavya Bhavan se prepared first Budget ke roop mein three-duty framework par based tha. [Q5]\n• Union Budget 2023-24 ki seven priorities ko 'Saptarishi' kaha gaya: Inclusive Development, Reaching the Last Mile, Infrastructure &\n Investment, Unleashing the Potential, Green Growth, Youth Power aur Financial Sector. [Q6]\n• Union Budget 2022-23 ki four priorities PM GatiShakti, Inclusive Development, Productivity Enhancement & Investment/Sunrise\n Opportunities/Energy Transition/Climate Action, aur Financing of Investments thi; disinvestment separate priority nahi tha. [Q7]\n• Union Budget 2017-18 ka TEC agenda 'Transform, Energise and Clean India' tha; 'Educate India' TEC ka part nahi tha. [Q8]\n• Union Budget 2021-22 mein Agriculture Infrastructure and Development Cess (AIDC) kuch selected items par propose hua; structure aisa\n rakha gaya ki most items par consumer burden unnecessarily na badhe. [Q9]\n• Economic Survey 2022-23 ne note kiya ki FY 2023 Budget highly uncertain macroeconomic environment mein present hua; baad ke\n geopolitical conflicts ne supply bottlenecks aur fuel-food-essential commodity prices par pressure badhaya. [Q10]\n• Economic Survey 2015-16 ne Indian economy ke socialism se limited marketism ki taraf move, lekin inefficient firms/activities ke exit\n mechanism ki weakness ko 'Chakravyuha Challenge' kaha. [Q11]\n• Union Budget 2023-24 mein States ko fiscal deficit up to 3.5% of GSDP allow tha, jisme 0.5% power-sector reforms se linked tha. Total\n expenditure estimate roughly Rs 45 lakh crore tha. [Q12]\n• Revised Estimates 2017-18 mein fiscal deficit 3.5% of GDP tha, jabki Budget Estimates 2017-18 mein 3.2% target tha. [Q13]\n• Fiscal deficit/GDP trend: 2017-18 3.5%, 2018-19 3.4%, 2019-20 4.6%, 2020-21 9.2%, 2021-22 6.7%, 2022-23 6.5%, 2023-24 5.5%, 2024-\n 25 4.8%, 2025-26 RE 4.4% aur 2026-27 BE 4.3%. [Q14]\n\n EXAM TRAP — Budget Data is Year-Specific\n ▸ Budget priorities, fiscal targets aur scheme themes year-specific hote hain; newer Budget/Economic Survey values purane years se differ\n kar sakte hain.\n [Q4, Q5, Q6, Q7, Q8, Q9, Q10, Q12, Q13, Q14]\n\n\n\n\n 2. GST — Structure, Council, Input Tax Credit & Administration\n\n• GST framework mein hulled cereal grains, cooked chicken eggs ke specified forms aur newspapers jaise essential items exempt category\n mein aa sakte hain; processed/canned fish aur processed shelled eggs par 5% GST category apply hoti hai. [Q15]\n• Central Government ne 2003 mein Vijay L. Kelkar ki chairmanship mein Task Force banaya; is Task Force ne Goods and Services Tax\n (GST) propose kiya. [Q16]\n• 101st Constitutional Amendment, 2016 se Article 246A add hua, jo Parliament aur State Legislatures dono ko GST par law banane ki\n concurrent power deta hai; inter-State supply par Parliament ki exclusive role bhi hai. [Q17]\n• GST 1 July 2017 se nationwide effective hua aur ye indirect tax hai. [Q18]","• GST implementation ke baad State-level VAT ka large part subsume/abolish hua; alcohol for human consumption jaise excluded items par\nState VAT framework continue karta hai. [Q19]\n• Constitution (101st Amendment) Act, 2016 ne GST ke constitutional provisions create kiye; GST 1 July 2017 se implement hua.\nAmendment Bill initially 122nd Constitutional Amendment Bill ke roop mein introduce hua tha. [Q20]\n• 101st Constitutional Amendment Act, 2016 ne GST regime implement karne ke liye required constitutional framework provide kiya. [Q21]\n• GST ko pehle April 2012 se introduce karne ka proposal tha; actual nationwide implementation 1 July 2017 se hua. [Q22]\n• GST ek destination-based indirect tax hai jo goods aur services ki supply par lagta hai aur India ko unified common market banane ka\nframework provide karta hai. [Q23]\n• 101st Amendment se Article 279A add hua aur GST Council ka provision bana. GST Council ka Chairperson Union Finance Minister hota\nhai; Union Minister of State for Finance/Revenue bhi member hota hai. [Q24]\n• GST Council ka Chairperson Union Finance Minister hota hai. [Q25]\n• GST ne multiple Central aur State indirect taxes ko subsume kiya, jisme Central Excise Duty, Additional Excise Duties, Service Tax aur\nseveral State VAT/sales-tax type levies shamil the. [Q26]\n• GST ke outside major items mein alcohol for human consumption aur electricity shamil hain; petroleum products ko GST levy ke liye\nCouncil recommendation framework ke under rakha gaya. Ghee GST ambit mein taxable item hai. [Q27]\n• October 2020 mein gross GST collection Rs 1 lakh crore se upar tha; January 2026 mein monthly gross GST collection Rs 1.93 lakh crore\nse upar pahucha. [Q28]\n• Revenue Neutral Rate (RNR) ka concept aise GST rate structure ko refer karta hai jisse tax-system change ke baad overall government\nrevenue broadly neutral rahe. [Q29]\n• Input Tax Credit (ITC) GST ka core mechanism hai jo cascading 'tax on tax' ko reduce karta hai. Purchase/input par paid eligible GST ko\noutput/sales GST liability se adjust kiya ja sakta hai. [Q30]\n• Input Tax Credit registered person ke eligible inward supplies par paid GST ka credit hai. Taxable aur zero-rated supplies ITC eligibility\nmein aa sakti hain; exempt/non-taxable supplies par general ITC restrictions apply hoti hain. [Q31]\n• Project Saksham new indirect-tax network/system integration se related project tha; Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs ne 28\nSeptember 2016 ko approval diya. [Q32]\n\nEXAM TRAP — GST — Constitutional/Administrative Details\n▸ GST launch date, Council composition, exemptions, RNR, ITC aur Project SAKSHAM alag factual points hain; inhe ek hi period/value\nsamajhkar merge na karein.\n[Q15, Q16, Q17, Q18, Q19, Q20, Q21, Q22, Q23, Q24, Q25, Q26, Q27, Q28, Q29, Q30, Q31, Q32]\n\n\n\n\n3. Fiscal Policy — Authority, Objectives, Instruments & Stimulus\n\n• India mein fiscal policy primarily Ministry of Finance frame karta hai; monetary policy RBI ke Monetary Policy Committee ke through decide\nhoti hai. Finance Commission Centre aur States ke beech divisible tax revenue/resources sharing recommend karta hai. [Q33]\n• Long Term Fiscal Policy 1985-86 Budget Speech mein then Finance Minister V. P. Singh ne announce ki thi. [Q34]\n• Government expenditure control/management Ministry of Finance ke under aata hai. Ministry ke six departments hain: Economic Affairs,\nExpenditure, Revenue, Financial Services, DIPAM aur Public Enterprises. [Q35]\n• 'Department of Banking' naam se separate department Ministry of Finance ke current six departments mein nahi hai; banking-related\nfunctions Department of Financial Services ke under aate hain. [Q36]\n• Central Government ke revenue expenditure mein interest payments ka burden kaafi bada component hai; high interest burden\ndevelopment/investment ke liye available fiscal space ko reduce kar sakta hai. [Q37]\n• Fiscal policy government ke revenue, expenditure, taxation, borrowing aur deficit decisions ka framework hai. Iske major aims\nincome/wealth distribution improve karna, employment support karna aur price/economic stability maintain karna hain; inter-State trade\nregulation iska core instrument nahi hai. [Q38]\n• Fiscal policy government finance se related hai aur government income, expenditure, public debt aur deficit financing policies ko cover karti\nhai. [Q39]\n• Fiscal policy ke major instruments taxation, public expenditure, public debt aur deficit financing hain. [Q40]\n• Taxation fiscal policy ka key instrument hai. [Q41]\n• Government taxation, borrowing aur public expenditure ki size/structure change karke economic activity ko influence karti hai. Public\nexpenditure, taxation aur deficit financing fiscal-policy tools hain; policy interest rate monetary-policy tool hai. [Q42]\n• J. M. Keynes ne depression se economy ko nikalne ke liye expansionary fiscal policy aur higher public expenditure par emphasis diya. Unki\nfamous book 'The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money' 1936 mein publish hui. [Q43]\n• Fiscal/financial stimulus government ka expansionary action hota hai jisme recession ya economic slowdown ke dauran demand aur\neconomic activity boost karne ke liye spending, tax relief ya sector-specific incentives diye jaate hain. [Q44]\n• Recession ke dauran public projects par expenditure badhana aggregate demand aur employment support karne wala fiscal measure ho\nsakta hai. Recession generally real GDP contraction ke saath income, employment, production aur sales ko affect karti hai. [Q45]\n\n\n4. Budget Preparation, Economic Survey & Budgeting Methods\n\n• Budget formulation ke broad stages: drawing/disbursing inputs, departmental estimates, scrutiny-consolidation, Ministry of Finance review,\ndisputes settlement, final consolidation aur Cabinet approval. [Q46]\n• Union Budget preparation aur Parliament mein presentation ke coordination ki nodal responsibility Ministry of Finance ke Department of\nEconomic Affairs ke Budget Division ke paas hoti hai. [Q47]","• Economic Survey Ministry of Finance prepare karta hai aur Union Budget se pehle Parliament mein table hota hai; ye previous year ki\neconomic performance aur policy issues ka review deta hai. [Q48]\n• Annual Economic Survey/Review Ministry of Finance prepare karta hai. [Q49]\n• Economic Survey officially Ministry of Finance ke Department of Economic Affairs se publish hota hai aur economy ka annual\npolicy/performance review present karta hai. [Q50]\n• Economic Survey every year Department of Economic Affairs, Ministry of Finance se publish hota hai aur macroeconomic trends, sector\nperformance aur policy issues review karta hai. [Q51]\n• Publication-institution mapping: Report on Currency and Finance—RBI; National Accounts Statistics—NSO; Economic Survey—Ministry of\nFinance; Wholesale Price Index—Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade/Ministry of Commerce & Industry framework.\n[Q52]\n• Budget government ki fiscal policy ka annual financial statement hai, jisme financial year 1 April-31 March ke estimated receipts aur\nexpenditure dikhaye jaate hain. [Q53]\n• Zero-Based Budgeting (ZBB) mein har activity/scheme ko previous allocation automatically continue karne ke bajay zero base se justify\nkiya jaata hai; expenditure se pehle necessity, utility aur productivity evaluate hoti hai. [Q54]\n• India mein Zero-Based Budgeting 1983 mein Department of Science & Technology mein start hui aur 1986-87 se ministries mein wider\nadoption hua. Har scheme ko fresh justification ke saath review kiya jaata hai. [Q55]\n• Budget 2023-24 allocations mein PM-JAY Rs 7,200 crore, Department of Health & Family Welfare Rs 86,175 crore, Ministry of Health &\nFamily Welfare Rs 89,155 crore aur Department of Health Research Rs 2,980 crore the. [Q56]\n• Union Budget 2011-12 mein Energy sector ko Rural Development, Social Services aur Transport se zyada allocation mila tha. [Q57]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein Transport Rs 5,98,520 crore, Rural Development Rs 2,73,108 crore, Energy Rs 1,09,029 crore aur Social\nWelfare Rs 62,362 crore allocate hua. [Q58]\n• Selected Union Budget allocations 2018-19 BE se 2025-26 BE tak increase hue: Defence 2.82 se 5.95 lakh crore, Agriculture & Allied 0.63\nse 1.63, Food Subsidy 1.69 se 2.28 aur Rural Development 1.39 se 2.73 lakh crore. [Q59]\n• Union Budget 2023-24 ke total expenditure breakup mein Interest Payments 20%, States' share in taxes/duties 18%, Defence 8% aur\nSubsidies 7% the. [Q60]\n\n\n5. Public Receipts & Expenditure — Revenue/Capital Classification\n\n• Government receipts do broad categories mein divide hote hain: Revenue Receipts aur Capital Receipts. Jo receipts government liability\ncreate karti hain ya financial assets reduce karti hain, unhe Capital Receipts kehte hain. [Q61]\n• Union Budget 2010-11 mein capital receipts revenue receipts se kam the; BE 2026-27 mein bhi revenue receipts Rs 35,33,150 crore\n(66.07%) aur capital receipts Rs 18,14,165 crore (33.93%) estimate hue. [Q62]\n• Income tax, public borrowing aur VAT/public taxes government revenue/receipts ke sources ho sakte hain; subsidy government\nexpenditure ka item hai. [Q63]\n• Corporation tax, PSU profits/dividends aur excise-type tax revenue account se linked hote hain; National Savings Certificates/small savings\naur World Bank loans capital receipts category mein aate hain. [Q64]\n• Revenue receipts mein tax revenue, interest receipts, government departments/PSUs ke profits-dividends shamil hote hain; small savings\ncapital receipts mein aati hain. [Q65]\n• Capital Budget government ke capital receipts aur capital expenditure ka account hai aur assets/liabilities mein changes ko capture karta\nhai. [Q66]\n• Old plan/non-plan framework mein subsidies, interest payments, defence, maintenance, education-health, pensions aur statutory transfers\njaise recurring items largely non-plan/revenue expenditure mein aate the. [Q67]\n• Old classification mein interest payments non-plan expenditure ka major item the; maintenance aur administrative expenses bhi non-plan\ncategory mein aate the. [Q68]\n• Interest payments old plan/non-plan classification mein non-plan expenditure ka important component the. [Q69]\n• 2017-18 se plan/non-plan expenditure classification abolish ho chuki hai; current Budget mein expenditure ko Revenue aur Capital\ncategories mein classify kiya jaata hai, jisme revenue expenditure larger component hai. [Q70]\n• Budget 2017-18 ke major reforms mein Budget presentation date ko 1 February karna, Railway Budget ko General Budget mein merge\nkarna aur Plan/Non-Plan expenditure classification end karna shamil tha. Centrally Sponsored Schemes ki sankhya badhana is reform\npackage ka part nahi tha. [Q71]\n• Railway Budget ko General/Union Budget ke saath 2017 se merge kar diya gaya. [Q72]\n• Railway Budget Union Budget mein Budget 2017-18 se merge hua. [Q73]\n• Budget deficit reduce karne ke liye revenue expenditure control aur revenue receipts increase karna helpful hota hai. New welfare spending\ndeficit badha sakti hai, jabki import duty cut revenue receipts ko reduce kar sakta hai. [Q74]\n• Fiscal deficit control ke common measures subsidy rationalisation, revenue expenditure restraint aur broader macro imbalances reduce\nkarna hain; uncontrolled public expenditure deficit ko badha sakta hai. [Q75]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein Union Government ke revenue expenditure ka largest item interest payments tha: around Rs 14,03,972\ncrore, roughly 3.57% of GDP. [Q76]\n• Union Budget ke revenue expenditure mein interest payment major/largest items mein se ek hota hai. [Q77]\n• Interest payment Revenue Expenditure ka item hai. BE 2026-27 mein iske liye Rs 14,03,972 crore allocate hua; 2025-26 BE/RE figures\naround Rs 12.76/12.74 lakh crore the. [Q78]\n• Union Government ke revenue account ka most important expenditure item interest payments hai. [Q79]\n• Union Government Budget mein interest payments historically sabse bade expenditure components mein rahe hain. [Q80]\n• 2008-10 period mein bhi aur BE 2026-27 mein bhi Central Government ke revenue/current expenditure ka major item interest payments\nraha; 2026-27 BE mein amount around Rs 14,03,972 crore tha. [Q81]","• 2015-16 mein old non-plan classification ke under interest payments largest item the. 2017-18 se plan/non-plan classification abolish ho\nchuki hai; BE 2026-27 mein bhi interest payments revenue expenditure ka largest component hain. [Q82]\n• Old non-plan expenditure classification mein interest payments sabse bada item the. [Q83]\nEXAM TRAP — Plan / Non-Plan Classification is Historical\n▸ Q67-Q83 mein historical Plan/Non-Plan classification aur older expenditure pattern use hua hai; 2017-18 se Plan/Non-Plan expenditure\nclassification end ho chuki hai.\n[Q67, Q68, Q69, Q70, Q71, Q72, Q73, Q74, Q75, Q76, Q77, Q78, Q79, Q80, Q81, Q82, Q83]\n\n\n\n\n6. Fiscal Deficit — Control Measures & Historical Targets\n\n• Fiscal deficit ko expenditure rationalisation, tax/revenue reforms aur institutional fiscal reforms se control kiya ja sakta hai; FDI promotion ya\nhigher-education privatisation direct fiscal-deficit control tools nahi hain. [Q84]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein fiscal deficit 4.3% of GDP estimate hua; 2025-26 BE aur RE dono mein 4.4% estimate tha. [Q85]\n• BE 2026-27 mein fiscal deficit 4.3% of GDP estimate hai; 2025-26 BE/RE figure 4.4% tha. Older 6.8% figure current estimate nahi hai.\n[Q86]\n• Union Budget 2024-25 mein fiscal deficit 4.9% of GDP estimate kiya gaya tha. [Q87]\n• Pandemic year 2020-21 mein Union fiscal deficit 9.2% of GDP tak pahucha; 2021-22 mein 6.7% raha. Revenue collections bhi recovery\nphase mein increase hui thi. [Q88]\n• FRBM framework ke fiscal-consolidation path mein Centre ka fiscal-deficit target around 3% of GDP tak lana tha; later escape clauses aur\nrevised fiscal paths ke karan timeline change hui. [Q89]\n• Union Budget 2000-01 mein fiscal deficit 5.1% of GDP budgeted tha; 2024-25 actual 4.8%, 2025-26 RE 4.4% aur 2026-27 BE 4.3%\nestimate hai. [Q90]\n• 2011-12 se 2014-15 ke four-year period mein fiscal-deficit/GDP ratio 2011-12 mein sabse zyada tha. 2017-18 se 2026-27 (BE) series mein\npeak 2020-21 mein 9.2% raha; 2021-22 mein 6.7%, 2022-23 mein 6.5%, 2023-24 mein 5.5%, 2024-25 mein 4.8%, 2025-26 (RE) mein 4.4%\naur 2026-27 (BE) mein 4.3% raha. [Q91]\n• 1990-91 mein fiscal deficit around 8.4% of GDP tha; Budget Estimate 2026-27 mein 4.3% estimate hai. [Q92]\n• Older context mein fiscal deficit around 5% of GNP/GDP ke aas-paas poocha gaya; current BE 2026-27 estimate 4.3% of GDP aur 2025-26\nRE 4.4% hai. [Q93]\n• FY 2024-25 actual fiscal deficit 4.8% of GDP raha; 2025-26 BE/RE mein 4.4% estimate hai. [Q94]\n• Union Budget 2009-10 mein fiscal deficit 6.8% of GDP budgeted tha. [Q95]\n• FY 2024-25 actual fiscal deficit 4.8% of GDP tha; 2025-26 Budget aur Revised Estimates mein 4.4% estimate hai. [Q96]\n• FY 2016-17 ka fiscal-deficit target aur actual roughly 3.5% of GDP tha; BE 2026-27 target 4.3% of GDP hai. [Q97]\n• P. Chidambaram ke 1997-98 'Dream Budget' mein major direct aur indirect tax rates mein significant reductions ki gayi thi. [Q98]\n• States ka combined fiscal deficit 2025-26 BE mein lagbhag Rs 11.8 lakh crore, around 3.3% of GDP estimate hua. [Q99]\n• Uttar Pradesh Budget 2023-24 ka total size Rs 6,90,242.43 crore tha aur fiscal deficit Rs 84,883.16 crore estimate hua. [Q100]\n• Union Budget mein external debt current exchange rates par value hota hai, historical exchange rates par nahi. High government borrowing\nreal interest rates ko elevated rakh sakti hai, rising fiscal deficit private investment ko crowd out kar sakta hai, aur interest payments non-plan\nrevenue expenditure ka major component rahe hain. [Q101]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Fiscal-Deficit Figures Change by Year\n▸ 1990s se 2024-25 tak fiscal-deficit target/actual figures ko ek doosre se mix mat karo; har figure apne year ke context mein hai.\n[Q85, Q86, Q87, Q88, Q89, Q90, Q91, Q92, Q93, Q94, Q95, Q96, Q97, Q98, Q99, Q100]\n\n\n\n\n7. Deficit Concepts — Fiscal, Revenue, Primary & Effective Revenue\n\n• Central Government ke major deficit measures mein Revenue Deficit, Fiscal Deficit aur Primary Deficit shamil hain. FY 2024-25 actual\nRevenue Deficit Rs 5,64,296 crore (1.7% GDP) tha; 2026-27 BE mein Rs 5,92,344 crore (1.5% GDP) estimate hai. [Q102]\n• Government Budget ke deficit measures mein Fiscal Deficit sabse comprehensive measure maana jaata hai, kyunki ye total borrowing\nrequirement reflect karta hai. [Q103]\n• Fiscal Deficit generally Revenue Deficit se bada hota hai. Revenue Deficit = Revenue Expenditure - Revenue Receipts; Fiscal Deficit =\nTotal Expenditure - non-debt receipts. [Q104]\n• Primary Deficit nikalne ke liye Fiscal Deficit se Interest Payments deduct kiye jaate hain. [Q105]\n• Traditional definition mein Fiscal Deficit ko budgetary deficit plus net internal aur external borrowings/liabilities ke broader requirement ke\nroop mein explain kiya jaata tha. [Q106]\n• Fiscal Deficit government ki total borrowing requirement ko capture karta hai, jisme market borrowings aur other liabilities shamil hoti hain.\n[Q107]\n• Fiscal Deficit = Total Expenditure - (Revenue Receipts + Recovery of Loans + Disinvestment/other non-debt capital receipts). [Q108]\n• Gross Fiscal Deficit ko financing side se Net Domestic Borrowing + RBI borrowing/monetisation + Foreign Borrowing ke through express\nkiya ja sakta hai. Domestic borrowing mein government securities, small savings aur banking-system financing shamil ho sakte hain. [Q109]\n• Fiscal Deficit batata hai ki non-debt receipts ke baad government ko expenditure finance karne ke liye kitni additional borrowing chahiye.\n[Q110]","• Fiscal Deficit budgetary gap se broader concept hai, kyunki ye government ki total borrowing requirement aur liabilities ko capture karta hai.\n[Q111]\n• Primary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit - Interest Payments. Isliye Fiscal Deficit = Primary Deficit + Interest Payments. [Q112]\n• Primary Deficit mein Interest Payments add karne par Fiscal Deficit milta hai. [Q113]\n• Agar Primary Deficit zero ho, to Fiscal Deficit exactly Interest Payments ke equal hota hai. [Q114]\n• Budget Estimates 2025-26 mein Primary Deficit around 0.8% of GDP estimate tha; 2024-25 BE/RE figures around 1.4% aur 1.3% the.\n[Q115]\n• Revenue Deficit tab hota hai jab Revenue Expenditure, Revenue Receipts se zyada ho. Is gap ko borrowing se finance karna current\nexpenditure cover karta hai aur directly capital asset create nahi karta. [Q116]\n• Effective Revenue Deficit = Revenue Deficit - Grants for Creation of Capital Assets. Ye concept Union Budget 2011-12 se introduce hua.\n[Q117]\n• Effective Revenue Deficit concept Union Budget 2011-12 mein introduce hua. [Q118]\n• Primary Deficit Fiscal Deficit se sirf Interest Payments minus karke milta hai; example mein Fiscal Deficit Rs 50,000 crore aur interest Rs\n1,500 crore ho to Primary Deficit Rs 48,500 crore hoga. [Q119]\n• Revenue Deficit Revenue Expenditure aur Revenue Receipts ka gap hai; Fiscal Deficit total expenditure aur total non-debt receipts ka gap\nhai, jise borrowing se finance kiya jaata hai. [Q120]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Deficit Formula Chain\n▸ Fiscal deficit, revenue deficit, primary deficit aur effective revenue deficit ke formulas mein receipts, expenditure aur interest-payment\ntreatment alag hota hai; formula terms ko mix na karein.\n[Q102, Q103, Q104, Q105, Q106, Q107, Q108, Q109, Q110, Q111, Q112, Q113, Q114, Q115, Q116, Q117, Q118, Q119, Q120]\n\n\n\n\n8. Government Borrowing, Deficit Financing & Subsidies\n\n• Household financial savings mein currency, bank deposits, debt securities, mutual funds, pensions, insurance aur small savings shamil\nhote hain. Government securities/Treasury Bills ke through household savings ka ek hissa government borrowing finance karta hai. [Q121]\n• Union Government ka fiscal deficit largely domestic debt/market borrowing se finance hota hai; Government Securities net debt receipts ka\nmajor source hain. [Q122]\n• Deficit financing agar new money creation ke through ho to money supply goods/services ke comparison mein faster badh sakti hai, jis se\ninflationary pressure create hota hai. [Q123]\n• Productive output ke matching increase ke bina excessive deficit financing inflationary pressure create kar sakti hai. [Q124]\n• Deficit financing se money supply increase ho sakti hai. [Q125]\n• Budget deficit ko new money create karke finance karne se market mein money supply badhti hai; goods supply same rahe to inflation\nincrease ho sakta hai. [Q126]\n• Developing economy mein deficit financing ka main objective development plans ko finance karke economic growth accelerate karna ho\nsakta hai; developed economies mein recession/depression counter karne ke liye bhi use hoti hai. [Q127]\n• Union Budget 2012-13 mein subsidy expenditure ko around 2% of GDP tak contain karne ka proposal tha. [Q128]\n• Subsidies government expenditure ka significant component hain aur fiscal deficit par pressure daal sakti hain; BE 2026-27 mein subsidies\noverall expenditure ka roughly 8.5% ke aas-paas thi. [Q129]\n• India mein subsidies ka long history hai; agricultural subsidy comparisons country-to-country differ karte hain aur several Western\neconomies bhi farmers ko substantial support deti hain. [Q130]\n\n\n9. Parliamentary Budget Procedure, Motions & Budgetary Control\n\n• Money Bill Article 110 ke matters se related hota hai, including Consolidated Fund of India se appropriation. Contingency Fund se\nappropriation Money Bill ki Article 110 defining category nahi hai. [Q131]\n• Demand for Grants par MPs Cut Motions move kar sakte hain: Policy Cut mein demand Rs 1 tak reduce; Economy Cut mein specified\namount reduce; Token Cut mein Rs 100 reduce karne ka proposal hota hai. [Q132]\n• Cut Motion Union Budget ki Demand for Grants ko reduce karne ka parliamentary motion hai. Types: Policy Cut, Economy Cut aur Token\nCut. [Q133]\n• Vote on Account full-year Budget demand pass karna nahi hai; ye Appropriation Act pass hone se pehle short period ke necessary\ngovernment expenditure ke liye advance grant allow karta hai. [Q134]\n• Vote on Account Budget/Appropriation Bill pass hone tak interim period ke expenditure ke liye funds appropriate karne ki temporary\nparliamentary authorization hai. [Q135]\n• Outcome Budget government spending ko measurable outputs/outcomes ke saath link karta hai, taaki citizens aur Parliament expenditure\nke results assess kar saken. [Q136]\n• Performance Budgeting ko USA mein Hoover Commission recommendations ke baad use kiya gaya; ye expenditure ko functions,\nprogrammes, performance aur cost-benefit results ke saath link karta hai. [Q137]\n• Ad hoc Treasury Bills India mein 1955 se temporary government financing ke liye RBI ko issue hote the. 31 March 1997 se ye system end\nhua aur Ways and Means Advances framework ne replace kiya. [Q138]\n• Public Accounts Committee government accounts aur CAG reports examine karti hai. Ismein 22 members—15 Lok Sabha aur 7 Rajya\nSabha—hote hain; conventionally Chairman opposition party se hota hai. [Q139]","EXAM TRAP — Budget Procedure Terms\n▸ Money Bill, policy cut, Vote on Account, Outcome Budget, Performance Budgeting aur parliamentary scrutiny alag concepts hain;\nkeywords ko interchange mat karo.\n[Q131, Q132, Q133, Q134, Q135, Q136, Q137, Q138, Q139]\n\n\n\n\n10. FRBM Act & Fiscal Responsibility Rules\n\n• FRBM review committee N. K. Singh ki chairmanship mein bani aur January 2017 mein report submit ki. [Q140]\n• Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act, 2003 26 August 2003 ko enact hua aur 5 July 2004 se effective hua; aim fiscal\ndiscipline aur consolidation ko legal framework dena tha. [Q141]\n• FRBM framework mein tax-GDP ratio improve karne ka fiscal target tha. BE 2026-27 mein gross tax revenue around 11.2% of GDP, 2025-\n26 RE 11.4% aur 2024-25 around 11.5% raha. [Q142]\n• FRBM Act mein Primary Deficit ko 2008-09 tak zero karne ki explicit statutory requirement nahi thi. [Q143]\n• FRBM Act ka major objective government finance mein fiscal discipline, transparency aur medium-term consolidation ensure karna hai.\n[Q144]\n\nEXAM TRAP — FRBM — Target vs Act Provision\n▸ FRBM ke debt/fiscal targets amendments ke saath change hue; enactment year, effective date aur target years ko alag context mein\npadho.\n[Q140, Q141, Q142, Q143, Q144]\n\n\n\n\n11. Disinvestment Targets & Railway Budget\n\n• Union Budget 2020-21 mein disinvestment receipts target Rs 2.1 lakh crore tha, jisme public-sector banks/financial institutions se around\nRs 90,000 crore target shamil tha. [Q145]\n• Union Budget 2019-20 mein PSU disinvestment target Rs 1.05 lakh crore tha. BE 2026-27 mein disinvestment/asset monetisation related\nother receipts target Rs 80,000 crore estimate hua. [Q146]\n• 1991 liberalisation ke baad CPSE disinvestment programme expand hua. FY 2000-01 mein Rs 10,000 crore target ke against around Rs\n1,829 crore realised hua. [Q147]\n• Railway Budget 2016 mein R&D organisation SRESTHA—Special Railway Establishment for Strategic Technology and Holistic\nAdvancement—form karne ka proposal diya gaya. [Q148]\n• Railway Budget 2013-14 mein premium passenger experience ke liye 'Anubhuti' coaches/train service concept approve hua. [Q149]\n\n\n12. Direct Tax Policy, DTC, Equalisation Levy, Capital Gains & Tax-GDP\n\n• Direct Tax Code (DTC) direct taxes, especially income tax laws ko simplify, consolidate aur modernise karne ka proposed legal framework\ntha. [Q150]\n• India mein Expenditure Tax ka suggestion economist Nicholas Kaldor ne diya; unhone wealth, gift aur estate-duty type taxes par bhi\nrecommendations di. Expenditure Tax April 1962 mein abolish hua. [Q151]\n• Indirect Transfer tab hota hai jab foreign company ke shares transfer hon aur unki substantial value India mein located assets se derive ho;\ntax law mein aise offshore share transfers bhi India-linked capital gains trigger kar sakte hain. [Q152]\n• India ne 2016 mein digital advertising payments par 6% Equalisation Levy introduce/propose ki, non-resident digital-service providers ko\ntax net mein lane ke objective se. [Q153]\n• Inherited immovable property bechne par holding period aur acquisition/inheritance rules ke basis par Capital Gains Tax liability arise ho\nsakti hai. [Q154]\n• Capital Gain asset/property ki value increase se sale/transfer par realise hone wala gain hai; product ki sales quantity badhna capital gain\nnahi hota. [Q155]\n• BE 2026-27 mein Income Tax receipts Rs 14,66,000 crore, around 3.73% of GDP estimate hui; 2025-26 RE mein Rs 13,12,000 crore\naround 3.34% tha. [Q156]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein direct taxes ka GDP share roughly 6.9% estimate hai; earlier periods mein ye around 5% ke level par tha.\n[Q157]\n• India ka gross Tax-GDP ratio BE 2026-27 mein 11.2% estimate hai; 2024-25 mein around 11.5% tha. [Q158]\n• Financial year 2021-22 mein India ka gross Tax-GDP ratio around 11.5% tha. [Q159]\n• Older 2008-09 context mein combined/particular tax-GDP measure around 16.39% estimate report hua tha; metric definition ko period\ncontext ke saath read karna chahiye. [Q160]\n• Tax-GDP ratio tab decline ho sakta hai jab tax revenue stable rahe aur GDP grow kare, ya dono grow karein lekin GDP ki growth tax\nrevenue se faster ho. [Q161]\n• Progressive taxation income redistribution ka strong tool hai: income badhne ke saath tax rate badhta hai, isliye higher-income groups\nrelatively zyada tax pay karte hain. Progressive public expenditure ke saath redistribution effect aur strong ho sakta hai. [Q162]\n• Degressive taxation progressive aur proportional systems ka mix hai: lower/middle ranges mein tax rate income ke saath increase karta\nhai, lekin ek level ke baad progression flatten ho sakti hai. [Q163]","• Indirect taxes ko regressive effect wala maana jaata hai kyunki same commodity-tax rate poor aur rich consumers dono par apply hota hai;\nlow-income household ki income ka larger proportion tax mein chala sakta hai. [Q164]\n• National-level Income Tax rates Union Government decide/impose karta hai. Income Tax direct tax hai jo individuals/HUFs/entities ki\ntaxable income par lagta hai. [Q165]\n• Union Budget 2016-17 mein annual income Rs 1 crore se above par surcharge rate 15% rakha gaya tha. [Q166]\n• Union Budget 2011-12 mein 80 years ya usse zyada age walon ke liye 'Very Senior Citizen' category create hui; us period mein Rs 5 lakh\ntak annual income tax-free threshold tha. [Q167]\n• Union Budget 2005-06 mein senior citizens ke liye tax-free income limit Rs 1.50 lakh thi. Budget 2011-12 mein 60-80 years ke liye Rs 3\nlakh aur 80+ very senior citizens ke liye Rs 5 lakh threshold rakha gaya. [Q168]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Tax Rates & Thresholds Change Over Time\n▸ Income-tax surcharge, senior-citizen limits aur Tax-GDP ratios year-specific facts hain; current rates ko older-year figures par apply na\nkarein.\n[Q156, Q157, Q158, Q159, Q160, Q161, Q162, Q163, Q164, Q165, Q166, Q167, Q168]\n\n\n\n\n13. Union Tax Revenue, Tax Sharing & Centre-State Tax Powers\n\n• Central Sales Tax inter-State trade par Union law ke under applicable tha; rate framework Centre set karta tha, collection generally\noriginating State karti thi. SEZ transactions ke liye specified exemptions available the. [Q169]\n• 2013-14 se 2016-17 ke four-year period mein Central Government ke gross tax revenue ki growth 2016-17 mein sabse zyada thi. 2017-18\nse 2026-27 (BE) series mein 11.5% growth 2021-22, 2023-24 aur 2024-25 mein record hui. [Q170]\n• Older Union revenue structure mein Central Excise aur Customs major sources the; current structure mein Income Tax, Corporation Tax\naur GST largest tax-revenue sources hain. [Q171]\n• 1995-96 mein Central revenue receipts ke tax composition mein Customs ka contribution around 28.4% aur Excise around 41.2% tha; ye\nhistorical-year figures hain. [Q172]\n• Older tax-source order Corporation Tax > Income Tax > Union Excise > Service Tax tha; BE 2026-27 mein Income Tax > Corporation Tax\n> GST > Union Excise > Customs major order hai. [Q173]\n• 2020-21 tax-share figures mein Corporation Tax 18%, Income Tax 17%, Union Excise 7% aur Customs 4% the; BE 2026-27 mein Income\nTax 21%, Corporation Tax 18%, GST/other taxes around 15%, Excise 6% aur Customs 4% ke order mein hain. [Q174]\n• Older data mein Corporation Tax > Income Tax > Union Excise > Customs order tha; BE 2026-27 mein Income Tax > Corporation Tax >\nUnion Excise > Customs ho gaya. [Q175]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein major tax-revenue order Income Tax > Corporation Tax > GST > Union Excise Duty > Customs Duty hai.\n[Q176]\n• BE 2026-27 tax estimates: Income Tax Rs 14,66,000 crore; Corporation Tax Rs 12,31,000 crore; GST Rs 10,19,020 crore; Union Excise\nRs 3,88,910 crore; Customs Rs 2,71,200 crore. [Q177]\n• Older Budgets mein Central Excise major tax source thi; current structure mein Income Tax, Corporation Tax aur GST sabse bade sources\nhain. [Q178]\n• BE 2026-27 mein Central tax revenue ka largest source Income Tax Rs 14,66,000 crore estimate hai; uske baad Corporation Tax Rs\n12,31,000 crore aur GST Rs 10,19,020 crore. [Q179]\n• Union Budget 2005-06 mein Corporation Tax largest tax-receipt source tha. [Q180]\n• Gross tax revenue mein Union Excise Duty ka relative share long term mein significantly decline hua hai. [Q181]\n• Road Tax primarily State Governments levy karte hain; ye Union Government ka general tax nahi hai. [Q182]\n• Constitutional scheme mein specified stamp duties Union levy kar sakta hai, lekin collection/appropriation States ke through hoti hai. [Q183]\n• Central taxes ka divisible pool Finance Commission ki recommendations ke basis par States ke saath share hota hai; tax-sharing structure\ntime ke saath amendments aur Finance Commission awards se change hua hai. [Q184]\n• Income Tax Union levy aur collect karta hai, lekin divisible-pool provisions ke under net proceeds ka share Centre aur States mein Finance\nCommission recommendations ke basis par distribute hota hai. [Q185]\n• Customs Duty aur Corporation Tax Union subjects hain. Sales/VAT-type taxes aur land revenue jaise several taxes State jurisdiction mein\nrahe; wealth/gift/estate-duty treatment historical tax law ke period ke saath change hua hai. [Q186]\n• Income Tax, Wealth Tax aur Estate Duty direct-tax examples hain, kyunki impact/incidence same taxpayer par hota hai. Sales Tax indirect\ntax hai, jiska burden seller se consumer ko shift ho sakta hai. [Q187]\n• Historical Union tax structure mein Excise Duty Centre ka major tax-income source thi. [Q188]\n• Capital Gains do broad types mein classify hote hain—Short-Term aur Long-Term; holding period aur asset type ke basis par tax treatment\ndecide hota hai. [Q189]\n• Company ke taxable profit/income par Corporation/Corporate Tax lagta hai. India mein 1960-61 se Company Tax ne earlier 'Super Tax'\nterminology/framework ko replace kiya. [Q190]\n• Corporation Tax Union Government levy aur collect karta hai aur historically Union tax category mein aata hai. [Q191]\nEXAM TRAP — Q172 — No Exact Numerical Match\n▸ 1995-96 mein Central revenue income mein Customs ka contribution 28.4% aur Excise ka contribution 41.2% tha.\n[Q172]","14. MAT, MODVAT, CENVAT, VAT & Service Tax\n\n• Minimum Alternate Tax (MAT) Budget 1996-97 mein introduce hua, taaki profitable companies exemptions/deductions use karke zero ya\nvery low tax liability na dikha saken. [Q192]\n• MODVAT 1 March 1986 se V. P. Singh ke tenure mein introduce hua; iska full form Modified Value Added Tax tha aur ye Central Excise\ncredit mechanism se related tha. [Q193]\n• MODVAT (Modified Value Added Tax) Excise Tax/Central Excise se related tha. [Q194]\n• CENVAT (Central Value Added Tax) Central Excise Duty framework se related hai. [Q195]\n• VAT value-addition based multi-point indirect tax hai; production-distribution chain ke har stage par incremental value par tax lagta hai aur\nfinal burden consumer bear karta hai. Implementation State-level administration ke through hua. [Q196]\n• VAT mein tax production/distribution chain ke har value-addition stage par, final sale tak, apply hota hai. [Q197]\n• 1 April 2005 ke initial VAT rollout mein several States ne immediately participate nahi kiya; Uttar Pradesh, Tamil Nadu aur some BJP-ruled\nStates initial holdouts the. [Q198]\n• December 2005 tak Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Uttar Pradesh aur Tamil Nadu ne VAT implement nahi\nkiya tha. [Q199]\n• India mein State-level VAT ka broad nationwide rollout 2005 se hua. [Q200]\n• Haryana India ka first State tha jisne State VAT system early implement kiya. [Q201]\n• GST 1 July 2017 se India bhar mein applicable hua, including Union Territories such as Andaman & Nicobar Islands aur Lakshadweep.\n[Q202]\n• Estate Duty Act 1953 mein enact hua; kuch older exam references first implementation ke liye 1957 figure use karte hain, isliye dono years\nko context ke saath distinguish karein. [Q203]\n• Service Tax Union Budget 1994-95 mein Raja J. Chelliah Committee recommendations ke background mein introduce hua; later GST mein\nsubsume ho gaya. [Q204]\n• Service Tax indirect tax tha, FY 1994-95 se introduce hua aur Union List subject tha; 2017 mein GST regime mein subsume ho gaya.\n[Q205]\n• Service Tax India mein 1994-95 mein introduce hua. [Q206]\n• Union Budget 2006-07 mein Service Tax rate 10% se 12% ki gayi; later Service Tax GST framework mein subsume ho gaya. [Q207]\n• Service Tax ka historical rate 12% raha; 1 July 2017 se ye GST mein subsume ho gaya. [Q208]\n• Service Tax ab separate levy ke roop mein general GST-covered services par apply nahi hota; GST regime mein subsume ho chuka hai.\n[Q209]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Pre-GST Tax Regimes\n▸ MAT, MODVAT, CENVAT, VAT aur Service Tax historical tax-regime concepts hain; applicable period aur replacement sequence ko\nclearly distinguish karo.\n[Q192, Q193, Q194, Q195, Q196, Q197, Q198, Q199, Q200, Q201, Q202, Q203, Q204, Q205, Q206, Q207, Q208, Q209]\n\n\n\n\n15. Direct vs Indirect Taxes, Miscellaneous Taxes & Disclosure Schemes\n\n• India mein Income Tax 24 July 1860 ko James Wilson ne introduce kiya. Modern Income Tax annual taxable income par direct tax hai.\n[Q210]\n• Income Tax direct tax hai, isliye iska legal burden commodity price mein indirect tax ki tarah automatically pass-through nahi hota. [Q211]\n• Imported/exported goods par Union Government jo levy lagata hai use Customs Duty kehte hain. [Q212]\n• Direct Tax ka burden generally same person par legally impose aur bear hota hai; examples: Income Tax, Corporation Tax, Capital Gains\nTax aur historical Wealth/Estate taxes. [Q213]\n• Excise Duty domestically produced goods ke manufacture/production par lagti thi. Excise, Sales Tax, Customs aur Service Tax indirect\ntaxes the; Income Tax aur Corporation Tax direct taxes hain. [Q214]\n• Estate Duty direct tax ka example hai. [Q215]\n• Income Tax direct tax hai, indirect tax nahi. [Q216]\n• Customs Duty aur Excise Duty indirect taxes hain; Corporation Tax aur historical Wealth Tax direct taxes hain. [Q217]\n• Excise Duty production/manufacture par lagne wala indirect tax tha; Sales Tax, Customs aur Service Tax bhi indirect-tax category mein\naate the, jabki Income Tax aur Corporation Tax direct taxes hain. [Q218]\n• Fringe Benefit Tax, Securities Transaction Tax aur Interest Tax historical classification mein direct-tax category se associated the. [Q219]\n• Sales Tax goods ki sale/purchase par lagne wala indirect tax hai; burden consumer tak shift ho sakta hai. Historically Central Sales Tax\ninter-State transactions aur State Sales Tax intra-State sales par apply hote the. [Q220]\n• Excess-profit tax, MAT aur Corporation/Company Tax corporate profits/income se linked hain; Capital Gains Tax asset transfer se arising\ngains par separate tax category hai. [Q221]\n• Entertainment Tax traditional constitutional framework mein State List subject tha; GST ke baad many entertainment levies subsume ho\ngaye, though local-body levies specified cases mein continue kar sakte hain. [Q222]\n• Corporation Tax Union Government levy karta hai, State Governments nahi. [Q223]\n• Customs Duty State revenue tax nahi, Union levy hai jo imported/exported goods par lagti hai. [Q224]\n• Alcoholic liquor for human consumption par State Excise Duty State Governments levy karte hain; ye State List taxation power se linked\nhai. [Q225]\n• Commodity Transaction Tax Union Budget 2013-14 mein introduce hua. [Q226]","• Telecom AGR (Adjusted Gross Revenue) licence fee aur spectrum-usage charge calculation ka base hai. DoT definition mein telecom ke\nsaath specified non-telecom revenues bhi AGR mein include ho sakte hain. [Q227]\n• Income Tax Department ne 27 October 2015 ko PAN Camp aur e-Sahyog initiatives launch kiye, Digital India framework ke under taxpayer\nservices improve karne ke objective se. [Q228]\n• Payment of Gratuity Act ke under statutory gratuity ceiling March 2018 se Rs 20 lakh hui; Central Government employees ke liye 1 January\n2024 se retirement gratuity ceiling Rs 25 lakh ki gayi. [Q229]\n• Voluntary Disclosure of Income Scheme (VDIS) 1 July 1997 se 31 December 1997 tak operate hui. [Q230]\n• Income Declaration Scheme 2016 ke close tak around Rs 65,250 crore undisclosed income declarations report hui thi. [Q231]\n\n\n16. Finance Commission — Constitutional Role, 15th & 16th Commissions\n\n• Constitution ke Article 280 ke under President generally every five years Finance Commission constitute karta hai. Commission divisible\ntaxes ke net proceeds ko Union aur States ke beech distribute karne aur State shares recommend karta hai. [Q232]\n• 16th Finance Commission 31 December 2023 ko notify hua. Iska mandate Union-State tax devolution, States ke shares, grants-in-aid\nprinciples aur Panchayats/Municipalities ke resources augment karne ke measures cover karta hai; disaster-management financing\narrangements ka review bhi ho sakta hai. Climate-management initiatives ki financing review specified mandate ka part nahi hai. [Q233]\n• Finance Commission ka constitutional provision Article 280 mein hai. [Q234]\n• Centre-State tax devolution aur financial distribution Finance Commission ki recommendations se guided hota hai. [Q235]\n• Finance Commission Union aur States ke beech divisible tax revenues ki distribution recommend karta hai. [Q236]\n• Old plan/non-plan grant framework mein several non-plan grants Finance Commission recommendations ke basis par States ko milte the.\n[Q237]\n• Article 280 ke under Finance Commission Union aur States ke beech divisible taxes ke net proceeds aur States ke inter-se shares\nrecommend karta hai. [Q238]\n• Planning Commission aur Finance Commission dono advisory nature ke bodies the/hain; unki recommendations automatically legally\nbinding nahi hoti. [Q239]\n• Finance Commission Article 280 se constituted constitutional body hai. 'Statutory body' terminology technically Act-created bodies ke liye\nuse hoti hai; Finance Commission ka primary classification constitutional body hai. [Q240]\n• Constitutional divisible-pool framework ke under Central taxes Finance Commission recommendations ke basis par States ke saath share\nhote hain. Agricultural Income Tax State taxation power hai, isliye ye Central divisible tax nahi hai. [Q241]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission 27 November 2017 ko N. K. Singh ki chairmanship mein constitute hua. Members mein Ajay Narayan Jha,\nAshok Lahiri, Anoop Singh aur Ramesh Chand rahe; Arvind Mehta Secretary the. [Q242]\n• Sixteenth Finance Commission 31 December 2023 ko Arvind Panagariya ki chairmanship mein constitute hua; iski recommendations 1\nApril 2026 se start five-year award period ke liye hain. [Q243]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission Article 280(1) ke under 27 November 2017 ko N. K. Singh ki chairmanship mein bana; isne 2020-21 aur\n2021-26 periods ke liye recommendations di. [Q244]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission ne 2022-23 se 2025-26 period mein States ke educational outcomes improve karne ke liye total Rs 4,800\ncrore performance-linked grant recommend ki. [Q245]\n• 15th Finance Commission award mein Bihar ka inter-se share divisible pool mein 2021-26 ke liye 10.058% tha; 14th FC mein around\n9.67%. 16th FC context mein Bihar ka share around 9.948% note kiya gaya. [Q246]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission ke 2021-26 devolution formula mein Bihar ka share 10.058% tha, jo States mein second-highest shares\nmein se ek tha. [Q247]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission ke horizontal devolution criteria: Income Distance, Population 2011, Area, Forest & Ecology, Demographic\nPerformance aur Tax/Fiscal Effort. Governance Reforms aur Stable Government separate criteria nahi the. [Q248]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Finance Commission — Commission-Specific Criteria\n▸ 15th Finance Commission ke 2021-26 devolution mein Bihar ka share 10.058% aur Uttar Pradesh ka 17.939% tha. 16th Finance\nCommission recommendations mein Bihar 9.948% aur Uttar Pradesh 17.619% hai.\n[Q232, Q233, Q234, Q235, Q236, Q237, Q238, Q239, Q240, Q241, Q242, Q243, Q244, Q245, Q246, Q247, Q248]\n\n\n\n\n17. Finance Commissions — 14th to 1st & Tax Devolution\n\n• Fourteenth Finance Commission (Y. V. Reddy) ne States ka vertical tax-devolution share 42% recommend kiya; Fifteenth ne 41% aur\nSixteenth award context mein bhi 41% retain hua. [Q249]\n• Fourteenth Finance Commission ne States ka divisible-pool share 32% se 42% tak increase kiya; grants/devolution ko award-period\ncontext ke saath read karna chahiye. [Q250]\n• Fourteenth Finance Commission ke Chairman Dr. Y. V. Reddy the. [Q251]\n• Fourteenth Finance Commission ne States ko divisible pool ka 42% recommend kiya; Thirteenth Finance Commission ka corresponding\nrecommendation 32% tha. [Q252]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission 13 November 2007 ko Dr. Vijay L. Kelkar ki chairmanship mein constitute hua. [Q253]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ki recommendations 2010-15 award period ke liye thi. [Q254]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ne States ka share divisible pool of Central taxes mein 32% recommend kiya. [Q255]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ka tax-devolution recommendation States ke liye 32% tha. [Q256]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ne Centre + States combined debt-GDP ratio ko 2014-15 tak 68% ke around reduce karne ka fiscal\nroadmap suggest kiya. [Q257]","• Thirteenth Finance Commission ke horizontal distribution criteria mein fiscal capacity/income distance ko largest weight mila. [Q258]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ne model GST aur compensation package design karne aur Central taxes ka share local bodies ko grants\nke roop mein earmark karne se related recommendations di. Agle 10 years mein millions of jobs create karne ka proposal uski distinctive\nrecommendation nahi tha. [Q259]\n• Finance Commission Chairmen mapping: Tenth—K. C. Pant; Eleventh—A. M. Khusro; Twelfth—C. Rangarajan; Thirteenth—Vijay Kelkar;\nFourteenth—Y. V. Reddy; Fifteenth—N. K. Singh; Sixteenth—Arvind Panagariya. [Q260]\n• First Finance Commission ke Chairman K. C. Neogy the. [Q261]\n• Finance Commission Chairmen: Ninth—N. K. P. Salve; Tenth—K. C. Pant; Eleventh—A. M. Khusro; Twelfth—C. Rangarajan. [Q262]\n• Twelfth Finance Commission ke Chairman C. Rangarajan the. [Q263]\n• Chairmen mapping: Eleventh—A. M. Khusro; Twelfth—C. Rangarajan; Thirteenth—Vijay L. Kelkar; Fourteenth—Y. V. Reddy. [Q264]\n• Twelfth Finance Commission ne States ka share shareable Central taxes mein 29.5% se 30.5% tak increase karne ki recommendation di.\n[Q265]\n• Twelfth Finance Commission ne Centre aur States ka Revenue Deficit 2009-10 tak eliminate karne ka roadmap recommend kiya; award\nperiod 2005-10 tha. [Q266]\n• Eleventh Finance Commission ne States ke liye Union taxes ka 29.5% share recommend kiya; specified sales-tax arrangements ke context\nmein adjustment provision bhi tha. [Q267]\n• Tenth Finance Commission ne States ke tax-devolution share ke liye around 29% recommend kiya; later 14th FC 42% aur 15th/16th\naround 41% tak pahuche. [Q268]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Finance Commission Percentages\n▸ 14th, 13th, 12th, 11th aur 10th Finance Commission ke tax-devolution percentages/criteria alag hain; adjacent figures ko mix mat karo.\n[Q249, Q250, Q251, Q252, Q253, Q254, Q255, Q256, Q257, Q258, Q259, Q260, Q261, Q262, Q263, Q264, Q265, Q266, Q267, Q268]\n\n\n\n\n18. Tax Reform Committees, Tax Relief, PAN & Regional Tax Holidays\n\n• Committee mapping: PSU disinvestment—C. Rangarajan; industrial sickness—Omkar Goswami; tax reforms—Raja J. Chelliah; insurance\nreforms—R. N. Malhotra. [Q269]\n• Vijay Kelkar Task Force tax reforms se related tha; deductions/exemptions rationalise karna aur tax structure simplify karna important\nrecommendations mein tha. [Q270]\n• Kelkar Committee ne Section 88 ke tax rebate ko abolish/rationalise karne ki recommendation tax-reform package ke part ke roop mein di.\n[Q271]\n• Tax-reform recommendations wali referred committee Kelkar Committee thi. [Q272]\n• Global Trust Bank ka merger Oriental Bank of Commerce mein hua tha, PNB mein nahi. Kelkar Committee ki later tax recommendations\nmein housing-loan relief par position evolve hui. [Q273]\n• 1991 Tax Reforms Committee ke Chairman Raja J. Chelliah the. [Q274]\n• Chelliah Committee direct aur indirect tax reforms se related thi. [Q275]\n• 1990s ke major tax reforms ka policy base Chelliah Committee recommendations ne provide kiya. [Q276]\n• National Savings Certificate, Public Provident Fund aur National Savings Scheme mein applicable tax-relief provisions the; Indira Vikas\nPatra mein same Income-Tax relief available nahi thi. [Q277]\n• Kisan Vikas Patra investment/interest ko same prescribed Income-Tax exemption nahi milti jo selected tax-saving small-savings\ninstruments ko mil sakti hai. [Q278]\n• PAN 10-character alphanumeric code hai: first 3 letters series, 4th holder status, 5th name/surname initial, next 4 digits serial sequence aur\nlast alphabetic check character hota hai. 4th-character examples: P individual, C company, H HUF, F firm, A AOP, T trust, B BOI, L local\nauthority, J artificial juridical person, G government. [Q279]\n• PAN Income Tax Department ka tax-identity document hai, jisme holder ka name, DOB aur PAN number hota hai; PAN card by itself\ncurrent residential-address proof nahi hai. [Q280]\n• 2001 Gujarat earthquake ke baad Kutch district ki eligible industries ke liye five-year excise-duty holiday announce hua tha. [Q281]\n• Union Budget 2000 mein North-Eastern Region ki eligible industrial units ke liye ten-year tax holiday announce kiya gaya. [Q282]\nEXAM TRAP — Committee Mapping\n▸ Kelkar, Chelliah aur other tax-reform committees ko chairman aur recommendation pair ke saath alag-alag yaad rakho; committee names\naur reform proposals mix na karein.\n[Q269, Q270, Q271, Q272, Q273, Q274, Q275, Q276]\n\n\n\n\n19. Local Finance, Fiscal Stimulus & NHAI Financing\n\n• Panchayat ke revenue sources mein local taxes/fees/fines, own income, grants, loans/assistance aur Finance Commission-recommended\ntransfers shamil hote hain. [Q283]\n• Gram Panchayat local fairs aur markets par specified local taxes/fees levy kar sakti hai; Sales Tax aur Land Revenue State-level\ntaxes/revenues hain. [Q284]\n• Recession ke dauran expansionary fiscal stimulus mein taxes reduce karna aur/or government expenditure increase karna aggregate\ndemand support karta hai; subsidy elimination normally expansionary stimulus nahi hai. [Q285]","• National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) 1988 ke Parliamentary Act se constitute hua aur February 1995 mein operational hua. NHAI\nfinancing ka important source fuel cess hai. [Q286]\n\n\n                               Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Fiscal Policy & Revenue Full Mind Map"];
